{
  "note": "Sensation and Sensory Perception",
  "source": "59682d1cd6d1",
  "script": "6592b33c3a89",
  "voice": "Patrick - Engaging and Warm",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "f05e4c24b84c",
  "duration": 165.46,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Sensation and Sensory Perception",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Sensation is the ability of the nervous system to transduce, encode and perceive information from external and internal stimuli.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 7.92
        },
        {
          "text": "It's worth unpacking those three words.",
          "start": 8.32,
          "end": 10.56
        },
        {
          "text": "Receptors convert the stimulus into electrical signals, and this is called transduction.",
          "start": 10.8,
          "end": 15.92
        },
        {
          "text": "The signals are then carried and processed as action potentials, which is known as encoding.",
          "start": 16.24,
          "end": 21.12
        },
        {
          "text": "Lastly, the cerebral cortex turns them into conscious experience, and that is perception.",
          "start": 21.52,
          "end": 27.04
        },
        {
          "text": "We'll go through the general senses first, move on to the special senses, and finish with the chain of neurons that carries sensory information up to the cortex.",
          "start": 27.52,
          "end": 36.24
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "General senses: somatic and visceral",
      "start": 37.094,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "To start with, the general sensory system is divided in two.",
          "start": 37.094,
          "end": 41.134
        },
        {
          "text": "On one side is somatic sensation, which arises from the body surface and its framework.",
          "start": 41.174,
          "end": 46.481
        },
        {
          "text": "It responds to mechanical, thermal and chemical stimulation.",
          "start": 46.507,
          "end": 50.334
        },
        {
          "text": "Somatic sensation is carried by somatic afferent fibres, and it includes modalities such as touch, temperature and pain.",
          "start": 50.374,
          "end": 57.854
        },
        {
          "text": "On the other side is visceral sensation, which arises from the internal organs.",
          "start": 57.894,
          "end": 62.734
        },
        {
          "text": "This kind of sensation generally does not reach the level of conscious awareness.",
          "start": 62.774,
          "end": 67.174
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Special senses",
      "start": 67.944,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Alongside these general senses stand the special senses.",
          "start": 67.944,
          "end": 71.904
        },
        {
          "text": "They are vision, hearing, taste, smell and balance.",
          "start": 71.944,
          "end": 76.624
        },
        {
          "text": "Rather than relying on the general sensory receptors of the skin and viscera, they are handled by dedicated sense organs.",
          "start": 76.664,
          "end": 83.704
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The three neuron relay to the cortex",
      "start": 84.48,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Now, sensation is perceived in the cerebral cortex.",
          "start": 84.48,
          "end": 88.2
        },
        {
          "text": "That means every sensory pathway must carry information from the site of sensation up to the somatosensory cortex.",
          "start": 88.24,
          "end": 95.733
        },
        {
          "text": "This is done by a chain of three neurons.",
          "start": 95.787,
          "end": 98.613
        },
        {
          "text": "The first-order neuron is the peripheral afferent itself, and its cell body sits in the dorsal root ganglia.",
          "start": 98.667,
          "end": 105.16
        },
        {
          "text": "Next comes the second-order neuron, which is located in the spinal cord or the brain stem.",
          "start": 105.2,
          "end": 110.4
        },
        {
          "text": "Finally, the third-order neuron is located in the thalamus, which relays the signal to the somatosensory cerebral cortex.",
          "start": 110.48,
          "end": 118
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Summary",
      "start": 118.544,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "To sum up, sensation transduces, encodes and perceives external and internal stimuli.",
          "start": 118.544,
          "end": 125.344
        },
        {
          "text": "The general senses split into somatic and visceral sensation.",
          "start": 125.384,
          "end": 129.224
        },
        {
          "text": "Somatic sensation responds to mechanical, thermal and chemical stimuli, while visceral sensation comes from the internal organs and is mostly unconscious.",
          "start": 129.264,
          "end": 138.704
        },
        {
          "text": "Both of these general senses are distinct from the special senses.",
          "start": 138.784,
          "end": 142.664
        },
        {
          "text": "Information reaches the cortex through a relay of three neurons.",
          "start": 142.704,
          "end": 146.651
        },
        {
          "text": "The first-order neuron comes from the periphery, with its cell body in the dorsal root ganglia.",
          "start": 146.677,
          "end": 151.877
        },
        {
          "text": "The second-order neuron lies in the spinal cord or brain stem, and the third-order neuron runs from the thalamus to the somatosensory cortex.",
          "start": 151.931,
          "end": 160.117
        },
        {
          "text": "With that, we've followed a sensation all the way from the receptor to conscious experience.",
          "start": 160.171,
          "end": 165.504
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
