    {"duration":34.063673,"alignment":{"characters":"Now, sensation is perceived in the cerebral cortex. That means every sensory pathway must carry information from the site of sensation up to the somatosensory cortex. This is done by a chain of three neurons. The first-order neuron is the peripheral afferent itself, and its cell body sits in the dorsal root ganglia. Next comes the second-order neuron, which is located in the spinal cord or the brain stem. Finally, the third-order neuron is located in the thalamus, which relays the signal to the somatosensory cerebral cortex.","starts":[0,53,107,160,320,400,720,760,800,840,880,920,960,1000,1040,1120,1200,1280,1360,1387,1413,1440,1493,1547,1600,1653,1707,1760,1840,1860,1880,1920,1947,1973,2000,2080,2200,2240,2280,2320,2380,2440,2500,2560,2640,3160,3264,3368,3472,3576,3680,3720,3760,3800,3840,3880,3920,4120,4160,4200,4240,4280,4320,4400,4448,4496,4544,4592,4640,4720,4800,4853,4907,4960,5013,5067,5120,5200,5280,5360,5440,5520,5600,5680,5760,5840,5900,5960,6020,6080,6240,6272,6304,6336,6368,6400,6720,6749,6778,6807,6836,6865,6895,6924,6953,6982,7011,7040,7120,7140,7160,7180,7200,7280,7307,7333,7360,7480,7510,7540,7570,7600,7640,7660,7680,7760,8200,8255,8310,8365,8420,8475,8530,8585,8640,8720,8800,8880,8920,8940,8960,9040,9067,9093,9120,9240,9280,9320,9360,9440,9520,9600,9680,9733,9787,9840,9920,10000,10080,10120,10160,10368,10576,10784,10992,11200,11253,11307,11320,11333,11347,11360,11440,11480,11520,11600,11660,11720,11780,11840,11880,11900,11920,12080,12120,12160,12208,12256,12304,12352,12400,12480,12560,12640,12720,12768,12816,12864,12912,12960,13040,13080,13120,13312,13504,13696,13888,14080,14133,14187,14204,14222,14240,14400,14432,14464,14496,14528,14560,14600,14640,14680,14720,14760,14800,14880,14920,14960,15040,15120,15200,15280,15320,15340,15360,15440,15467,15493,15520,15600,15653,15707,15760,15806,15851,15897,15943,15989,16034,16080,16160,16208,16256,16304,16352,16400,16453,16507,16560,16680,16740,16800,16940,