    {"duration":46.915918,"alignment":{"characters":"To sum up, sensation transduces, encodes and perceives external and internal stimuli. The general senses split into somatic and visceral sensation. Somatic sensation responds to mechanical, thermal and chemical stimuli, while visceral sensation comes from the internal organs and is mostly unconscious. Both of these general senses are distinct from the special senses. Information reaches the cortex through a relay of three neurons. The first-order neuron comes from the periphery, with its cell body in the dorsal root ganglia. The second-order neuron lies in the spinal cord or brain stem, and the third-order neuron runs from the thalamus to the somatosensory cortex. With that, we've followed a sensation all the way from the receptor to conscious experience.","starts":[0,80,160,240,293,347,400,480,560,640,880,920,960,1030,1100,1170,1240,1310,1380,1450,1520,1600,1664,1728,1792,1856,1920,2000,2080,2187,2293,2400,2560,2640,2747,2853,2960,3060,3160,3260,3360,3440,3467,3493,3520,3680,3733,3787,3840,3880,3920,4000,4080,4160,4240,4320,4400,4480,4560,4640,4720,4800,4880,4960,5080,5120,5160,5200,5280,5340,5400,5460,5520,5580,5640,5700,5760,5840,5900,5960,6020,6080,6187,6293,6400,6800,6840,6853,6867,6880,6960,7006,7051,7097,7143,7189,7234,7280,7360,7440,7504,7568,7632,7696,7760,7840,7888,7936,7984,8032,8080,8160,8220,8280,8340,8400,8587,8773,8804,8836,8867,8898,8929,8960,9080,9120,9160,9200,9280,9307,9333,9360,9480,9510,9540,9570,9600,9640,9680,9800,9920,10040,10160,10280,10400,10520,10640,10680,10720,10920,10953,10987,11020,11053,11087,11120,11160,11200,11260,11320,11380,11440,11500,11560,11620,11680,11760,11817,11874,11931,11989,12046,12103,12160,12200,12240,12280,12320,12400,12440,12480,12520,12560,12600,12640,12740,12840,12940,13040,13120,13320,13349,13377,13406,13434,13463,13491,13520,13680,13733,13787,13840,13920,13960,14000,14040,14080,14120,14160,14200,14240,14320,14380,14440,14500,14560,14747,14933,15120,15160,15200,1